# Optional proof providers sit off the side

This becomes beautifully modular:

```text
VerificationResult
       │
       ├── deterministic verifier
       ├── Inspect evaluator
       ├── human reviewer
       ├── platform receipt
       ├── Sigstore
       ├── dstack TEE
       └── DeepProve zkML
```

DeepProve can prove a model inference from committed input/model to output; it should remain a **specific evidence provider**, not WorkerKit's execution substrate. ([Lagrange][9])

Similarly, dstack can prove that a particular workload/configuration ran in an attested confidential environment and can gate secret/key release based on that attestation. ([dstack][10])

ERC-8004 then becomes an optional publication target for these validation/reputation signals; its design explicitly supports pluggable validation approaches including zkML and TEE while leaving payment orthogonal. ([Ethereum Improvement Proposals][11])

---

# Storage architecture

Keep this equally small.

```text
WORKER MACHINE

SQLite WAL
    events
    outbox
    run projection
          │
          │ spool-first
          ▼
WORKERKIT SERVER

Postgres
    canonical events
    projections
    external receipts

R2 / S3
    content-addressed artifacts

          │
          ▼
periodic Merkle checkpoints

          │ optional
          ▼
Sigstore / chain anchor
```

The Builda spool-first pattern is worth copying: write locally before transmission and acknowledge only successfully accepted events.

I would **not use Temporal/DBOS as WorkerKit's canonical state store**.

If an underlying Worker uses DBOS, excellent. DBOS provides durable recovery, idempotent workflow IDs and checkpointed steps. ([DBOS Docs][12])

WorkerKit merely records:

```text
executor = dbos
execution_id = xyz
```

Same for Temporal.

